+                "x-opensip-ownership-attribute": "An ATTRIBUTE of the cell named by (capabilityId, languageMode, workspaceRoot), never part of its name. It decides whether a missing Coverage entry for that cell contributes indeterminate, so it must have exactly one value per cell."
               }
             }
           },
-          "x-opensip-order": "canonical-set"
+          "x-opensip-order": "canonical-set",
+          "x-opensip-uniqueness": {
+            "ownershipTuple": [
+              "capabilityId",
+              "languageMode",
+              "workspaceRoot"
+            ],
+            "law": "Within ONE analysis-spec the ownership tuple is UNIQUE. Two rows carrying the same (capabilityId, languageMode, workspaceRoot) are refused whatever their `required` values are; there is deliberately no last-wins, no order dependence and no merge of requiredness, so a caller states one value per cell or the record is refused.",
+            "why": "The tuple names one cell of work for one unit and `required` is that cell's attribute. Two disagreeing rows leave requiredness undetermined for a cell whose value decides an indeterminate contribution, and BOTH rows enter analysisSpecDigest and therefore PlanId - so the contradiction is committed, not transient. native section 1.4 already refuses a duplicate capabilityId in the RELEASE declaration for exactly this reason; this is the analysis-spec's own analogue, published here because this is the record's owning schema.",
+            "stillLegal": "The SAME capabilityId on a different workspaceRoot or a different languageMode is a different cell and remains legal, and different capabilities on one unit remain legal. Only the tuple is constrained.",
+            "notUniqueItems": "uniqueItems cannot express this: two rows differing only in `required` are DISTINCT items and it admits them. uniqueItems and the canonical-set order law own the WHOLE-ITEM duplicate, which refuses earlier and stays their refusal; this rule owns only distinct rows over one tuple.",
+            "admittedBy": "native_evidence_model.admit_requested_capabilities (internal key native.requested-capability-duplicate-ownership-tuple), which is called BOTH at the pre-Plan analysis-spec boundary and again at retained Run closure over the retained analysis-spec, so the two boundaries agree by construction.",
+            "publicRoute": "native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-public-route-registry/keys/native.requested-capability-duplicate-ownership-tuple - origin-dependent over the existing external-configuration / externally-supplied-spec / host-generated-internal-layer routes. No public DomainDetailCode member is added."
+          }
         },
         "policyPackIds": {
           "type": "array",
@@ -3680,6 +3694,20 @@
           }
         },
         "note": "An analysis-spec or stage-spec parameter may only cite a document on this closed list. That replaces the withdrawn \"a payload schema document must constrain the payload directly\" rule, which contradicted the native and workflow bundle registries. A cited schema with no row refuses: there is no default row, no caller-selected schema and no permissive fallback for an unregistered parameter.",
+        "selectionCardinality": {
+          "law": "ONE Plan selects AT MOST ONE parameter per registered row. Zero is legal for a row this analysis does not need; two or more entries of analysis-spec.parameters (or stage-spec.parameters) whose schemaDigest resolves to the SAME row are REFUSED, whatever their payloadDigests are. This is a SELECTION rule about how many entries a SPEC may carry and is a different question from keyIsTheDocumentNotThePair, which is about how many REGISTRY ROWS one document digest may resolve to; PAYLOAD_PARAMETER_AMBIGUOUS_ROW answers the second and never constrained the first.",
+          "why": "A parameter row is a Plan INPUT: the payload it cites is the value the analysis is run with. Two entries under one row put two different values in one input slot and nothing decides which is the Plan's. Both enter analysisSpecDigest and therefore PlanId, so two conforming hosts could bind different documents from ONE admitted Plan and the resulting comparison axis would attribute differently for the same PlanId. Refusing is the registry declining an arbitrary selection, exactly as it does for an ambiguous row. There is deliberately NO default, NO first-listed or last-listed winner, and NO merge.",
+          "atMostOnePerSpec": true,
+          "zeroIsLegal": "An ordinary analysis that does not need a scope-policy input selects no ScopeDocumentV1 parameter and closes. A caller that DOES need it - baseline adoption and comparison - still gets the explicit missing-selection refusal from the workflow binding verifier; absence is never silently substituted, and the foundation scope-descriptor named by plan.scopeDigest is a DIFFERENT record that never stands in for ScopeDocumentV1.",
+          "exactDuplicatesAreADifferentRefusal": "Two BYTE-IDENTICAL parameter entries are a malformed record, not an ambiguous selection: analysis-spec.parameters is uniqueItems with x-opensip-order canonical-set, so a whole-item duplicate refuses on the schema and on the collection order law (ORDER_OR_DUPLICATE) before any selection question is asked. This rule owns only the case those cannot see: DISTINCT entries citing one row.",
+          "enforcedAt": [
+            "the PRE-PLAN analysis-spec admission boundary (native_evidence_model.admit_analysis_spec, after schema and vocabulary admission)",
+            "retained Run closure over the retained analysis-spec (identity-model close_run, ANALYSIS_SPEC_PARAMETER_SELECTION_AMBIGUOUS)",
+            "independently in the bounded workflow scope binding verifier when it is called directly (workflows_model.verify_scope_parameter_binding)"
+          ],
+          "precedence": "MOST SPECIFIC FIRST. The import-source-context row keeps its own named guard IMPORT_SOURCE_CONTEXT_MULTIPLE where that guard runs - inside the Run-closure import derivation, which is the boundary that consumes the row - and this generic selection guard is the TOTAL backstop that runs for every row and every Plan, including one with no selected import. Nothing about the import row's zero/one/multiple rule changes: zero means an empty declared-build set, one is the selection, more than one is refused.",
+          "notAStageSpecDuplicate": "A stage-spec carries a SUBSET of the Plan analysis-spec parameters (STAGE_SPEC_HIDDEN_PARAMETER), so a Plan admitted under this rule cannot contain a stage that selects a row twice; the stage-spec case is implied rather than separately guarded, and stating that is not a claim that a second guard exists."
+        },
         "keyIsTheDocumentNotThePair": "Normative accounting of a bounded limitation, retained deliberately (CB4-ADV-4). The key is the cited schemaDigest, which is the SHA-256 of the whole DOCUMENT's bytes, not a (document, selector) pair. Both rows are distinguishable today - import-source-context.schema.json and policy-document.schema.json are different documents with different digests - so there is no ambiguity in this release. The limitation is structural and is stated rather than left to be rediscovered: a SECOND parameter row selecting another $def out of a document an existing row already cites would be indistinguishable by this key, and the first listed would otherwise silently win. That case is FORBIDDEN and refuses (PAYLOAD_PARAMETER_AMBIGUOUS_ROW), which is the registry law declining to make an arbitrary selection rather than a defect. Note that the same policy-document.schema.json bytes are also named by plan.policyDigest and plan.waiverDigest, but under the `canonical-record` representation and a different payload CLASS, so those are not in this key space and raise no collision.",
         "whyNotRekeyByDocumentAndSelector": "Re-keying the class by (document, selector) would lift the restriction and would change no identity today, and it is deliberately NOT done here. No existing requirement needs a second $def out of one document as a parameter; adding flexibility no requirement asks for would widen the closed key space and the admission surface for a case that does not exist, and would retire an explicit refusal in exchange for a rule that has never been exercised. If such a requirement ever arises it is an explicit versioned design correction with its own controls - not a silent widening, and not something an implementer may assume."
       }
